# ==============================================================================
# The AquariusOS boot animation
# ==============================================================================
# PLAIN ENGLISH
#
# This file is the front door of the AquariusOS boot screen — the screen you see
# for a few seconds between choosing AquariusOS in the boot menu and the login
# screen appearing, and again for a couple of seconds after you choose Shut Down.
#
# The program that draws it is called Plymouth. This file tells Plymouth two
# things and nothing else:
#
#   * which of its drawing plug-ins to use (`script` — see the long note below)
#   * where to find the two things that plug-in needs: the folder of pictures,
#     and the little program that plays them
#
# EVERYTHING ELSE — the animation, the colours, the positions, the words on the
# update screen, the disk-password box — is in aquarius.script, next to this
# file. That is where to go to change how the boot screen behaves.
#
# ⚠️ AFTER ANY CHANGE THE BOOT RAMDISK HAS TO BE REBUILT, or the machine keeps
# showing the old boot screen. The build does that for you in
# build_files/80-boot-branding.sh — you do not have to do anything, but if you
# ever wonder why an edit "did nothing", that is the mechanism, and
# docs/restart/boot-branding.md explains it.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ⚠️ WHY `script` AND NOT `two-step` — AND WHY THAT IS THE OPPOSITE OF WHAT THIS
#    FILE SAID BEFORE 6 SEPTEMBER 2026
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Plymouth themes are drawn by one of several plug-ins, and this file used to
# name a different one. The history matters, because the reasoning that chose
# the old one was sound and it is worth knowing what changed.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE SAID BEFORE. The theme used `two-step`, and for good reasons:
#
#   * `two-step` is the plug-in Fedora's OWN default themes use (`bgrt`, which a
#     stock Fedora machine boots with, and `spinner`). So it is the code path
#     that Fedora, Red Hat and Universal Blue test on every graphics card there
#     is, on every release — which matters most on NVIDIA, where the boot splash
#     is historically the first thing to break.
#
#   * It already knows how to draw the screens a boot splash occasionally has to
#     draw and that are easy to forget: the "type your disk password" box, the
#     shutdown screen, the "installing updates, do not turn the computer off"
#     screen. Those came free, and they were correct.
#
#   * The cost was that `two-step` can only play a fixed loop of pictures in one
#     place on the screen. It cannot tell the difference between starting up and
#     shutting down, and it cannot play something once and then stop.
#
# WHAT CHANGED. On 2026-09-06 Royce approved a designed boot ANIMATION rather
# than a still logo with a row of pulsing dots under it: AquariusOS is named
# after Aquarius, the water-bearer, so the "A" is POURED — a stream falls from
# above the screen, both legs of the A run downward out of it, the wave spills
# across, the name fades in — and on shutdown the wind takes the whole thing
# apart again and blows it away to the right.
#
# That is a STORY, and a story needs a sequence: it has to play once, in order,
# and then hold still. It also has to be a DIFFERENT story on the way down from
# the one on the way up. `two-step` cannot do either of those things — not with
# better pictures, not with different settings. Only the `script` plug-in can.
#
# Royce's call, in as many words: a story needs a sequence. So the trade is
# taken deliberately, with eyes open:
#
#   WHAT WE GIVE UP.  The three screens that used to come free now have to be
#     drawn by hand in aquarius.script. The one that matters is the disk-password
#     box — the one nobody would notice was missing until the day somebody turned
#     on disk encryption and the machine appeared to hang at a blank screen. It
#     is written, it is in that file under "THE DISK PASSWORD", and it is on the
#     bench-test list in docs/restart/boot-branding.md precisely because it is
#     the piece a build cannot prove.
#
#     THE UPDATE SCREEN IS KEPT, not dropped. "Installing updates — please do not
#     turn the computer off", the progress bar and the percentage are all drawn
#     by our own script now, with the same words this file used to carry.
#
#   WHAT WE GAIN.  The pour, the hold, the wind — and a boot screen that is our
#     own design rather than Fedora's design in our colours.
#
#   THE RISK WE ACCEPT.  `script` is a less-travelled code path than `two-step`.
#     It is not exotic — it ships in Fedora as `plymouth-plugin-script`, it is
#     what most custom boot themes in the world use, and Plymouth's own example
#     theme is written for it — but it is not what Fedora's default theme
#     exercises. If a graphics card ever shows a black screen where the animation
#     should be, this is the first thing to suspect, and `sudo
#     plymouth-set-default-theme spinner` is the one-line way to test that
#     theory.
# ==============================================================================

[Plymouth Theme]
Name=AquariusOS
Description=The Aquarius mark poured out of falling water, and blown away by the wind.

# The drawing plug-in. `script` means "run the little program named below and let
# it draw everything". It comes from the plymouth-plugin-script package, which
# build_files/20-hardware-media.sh installs and the build checks for by name.
ModuleName=script

[script]
# Where the pictures are. Plymouth looks for every picture the script asks for in
# this one folder: the 66 frames of the pour, the 57 of the wind, the hold, and
# the four small shapes the update and password screens are built from.
ImageDir=/usr/share/plymouth/themes/aquarius

# The little program that plays them. Everything the boot screen DOES is in
# there, written in plain steps with comments a beginner can follow.
ScriptFile=/usr/share/plymouth/themes/aquarius/aquarius.script

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHERE THE COMPUTER-MAKER'S BADGE WENT
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Modern computers leave a picture of their own logo sitting in memory when they
# hand control to the operating system (it is called the BGRT), and Fedora's
# default boot theme — literally named `bgrt` — exists to pick that picture up
# and use it as the background. That is why a stock Fedora machine shows an ASUS
# or Dell badge while it starts.
#
# The old two-step theme had to switch that off with a `UseFirmwareBackground`
# line in EVERY ONE of its sections, and miss one and the badge came back on that
# screen only.
#
# The `script` plug-in has no such setting AT ALL — it does not read the firmware
# picture and has no code to draw it. Verified by reading the plug-in's own
# source for the Plymouth version this image ships (24.004.60): the only two
# settings it reads out of this file are the two above. Our script paints its own
# flat Midnight ground over the whole screen on every single frame, so there is
# nothing for a badge to show through.
#
# So the badge is gone by construction here, rather than by remembering to switch
# it off in six places. The build checks that this file names no such setting.
